"""The longer telling of the big moments on the timeline, shown on their own event pages (src/eventpages.py).

MORE["English title of the event, exactly as in data.py"] = ([English paragraphs], [Welsh paragraphs]).
Follow EDITORIAL.md: proud and true, nothing negative about the people of Welsh history, Britons not Celts, kings are kings,
Arthur in the words of Welsh tradition.
"""

MORE = {
    "Neanderthals at Pontnewydd": (
        ["In the 1980s archaeologists digging in Pontnewydd Cave, in the Elwy valley, found teeth and pieces of bone from at least five people, children among them. They lived here about 230,000 years ago, in a warm spell between ice ages, and they were early Neanderthals.",
         "They made hand axes from the hard local stone and hunted horse, deer and bear in the valley. No older human remains have been found in Wales, and Pontnewydd is the farthest north and west that people of that age have been found anywhere in Europe. Wales has been a home to people for a very long time."],
        ["Yn y 1980au daeth archaeolegwyr a oedd yn cloddio yn Ogof Bontnewydd, yn nyffryn Elwy, o hyd i ddannedd a darnau o asgwrn o leiaf bump o bobl, a phlant yn eu plith. Roedden nhw’n byw yma tua 230,000 o flynyddoedd yn ôl, mewn cyfnod cynnes rhwng dau oes iâ, a Neanderthaliaid cynnar oedden nhw.",
         "Gwnaethant fwyeill llaw o’r garreg galed leol a hela ceffylau, ceirw ac eirth yn y dyffryn. Ni chafwyd olion dynol hŷn yng Nghymru, a Phontnewydd yw’r lle pellaf i’r gogledd a’r gorllewin yn Ewrop lle cafwyd hyd i bobl o’r oes honno. Bu Cymru’n gartref i bobl ers amser maith iawn."]),
    "The bluestones of Preseli": (
        ["The smaller stones of Stonehenge, the bluestones, came from Mynydd Preseli in Pembrokeshire. Geologists have matched them to the outcrops of Carn Goedog and Craig Rhos-y-felin, where the quarry workings of about 3000 BC can still be seen.",
         "Moving them some 150 miles to Salisbury Plain was one of the great feats of the Stone Age. Why they went is still asked: perhaps the hills of Preseli were holy, perhaps the stones were thought to heal. Whatever the reason, the first circle at Stonehenge was raised with stones from Wales."],
        ["Daeth cerrig lleiaf Côr y Cewri, y cerrig gleision, o Fynydd Preseli yn Sir Benfro. Mae daearegwyr wedi’u cysylltu â brigiadau Carn Goedog a Chraig Rhos-y-felin, lle gellir gweld olion y chwareli o tua 3000 CC o hyd.",
         "Roedd eu symud tua 150 milltir i Wastadedd Caersallog yn un o gampau mawr Oes y Cerrig. Mae pobl yn dal i ofyn pam: efallai fod bryniau’r Preseli yn sanctaidd, efallai y credid bod y cerrig yn iacháu. Beth bynnag y rheswm, codwyd y cylch cyntaf yng Nghôr y Cewri â cherrig o Gymru."]),
    "Caratacus speaks in Rome": (
        ["King Caratacus led the Britons against Rome for nine years. When the south fell he came west and fought on with the Silures of Gwent and the Ordovices of the north, until his last battle in about 51 AD. He sought shelter with Cartimandua, queen of the Brigantes, and was handed over to the Romans.",
         "Tacitus, the Roman historian, gives his speech before the emperor Claudius: he had horses, men, arms and wealth, and was it any wonder he was unwilling to lose them? If Rome wished to rule the world, did it follow that everyone should welcome slavery? Claudius pardoned him, his wife and his brothers. Caratacus is remembered as the first hero of the Britons whose own words have come down to us."],
        ["Arweiniodd y Brenin Caradog y Brythoniaid yn erbyn Rhufain am naw mlynedd. Pan syrthiodd y de daeth i’r gorllewin ac ymladd ymlaen gyda’r Silwriaid yng Ngwent a’r Ordoficiaid yn y gogledd, hyd ei frwydr olaf tua 51 OC. Ceisiodd loches gan Cartimandua, brenhines y Brigantes, a rhoddwyd ef yn nwylo’r Rhufeiniaid.",
         "Mae Tacitus, yr hanesydd Rhufeinig, yn rhoi ei araith gerbron yr ymerawdwr Claudius: roedd ganddo feirch, gwŷr, arfau a chyfoeth, ac a oedd yn syndod nad oedd am eu colli? Os oedd Rhufain am reoli’r byd, a oedd yn dilyn y dylai pawb groesawu caethiwed? Rhoddodd Claudius bardwn iddo, i’w wraig ac i’w frodyr. Cofir Caradog fel arwr cyntaf y Brythoniaid y daeth ei eiriau ei hun i lawr atom."]),
    "Rome attacks Ynys Môn": (
        ["Ynys Môn was the heart of Druid learning in Britain. In 60 AD the governor Suetonius Paulinus brought his army over the Menai Strait, the foot soldiers in flat-bottomed boats and the cavalry swimming beside their horses.",
         "Tacitus describes what met them on the shore: armed warriors, women in black robes with flaming torches, and Druids with their hands raised to the sky, calling down curses. The legions stood frozen for a moment before they attacked. The sacred groves were cut down, but news of Boudica’s rising called the army away before the island could be held, and Môn was not finally taken until 77 AD."],
        ["Ynys Môn oedd calon dysg y Derwyddon ym Mhrydain. Yn 60 OC daeth y llywodraethwr Suetonius Paulinus â’i fyddin dros y Fenai, y milwyr traed mewn cychod gwaelod gwastad a’r marchogion yn nofio wrth ochr eu ceffylau.",
         "Mae Tacitus yn disgrifio’r hyn a’u hwynebodd ar y lan: rhyfelwyr arfog, gwragedd mewn gwisgoedd duon â ffaglau ynghyn, a Derwyddon â’u dwylo i fyny i’r nef yn galw melltithion. Safodd y llengoedd yn stond am ennyd cyn ymosod. Torrwyd y llwyni sanctaidd, ond galwyd y fyddin ymaith gan newyddion am wrthryfel Buddug cyn y gellid dal yr ynys, ac ni chipiwyd Môn yn derfynol tan 77 OC."]),
    "The victory at Badon": (
        ["After Rome left, the Britons fought for their island against the Saxons. Gildas, writing in the sixth century, tells of the siege of Mount Badon, the great victory that brought a peace still unbroken in his own day.",
         "The History of the Britons, written in Wales in the ninth century, names the leader: Arthur, who carried the cross of Christ on his shoulders and won twelve battles, the last of them at Badon. Welsh tradition holds that he was Athrwys ap Meurig, king of Gwent and Glywysing. Historians still debate where Badon was and when it was fought."],
        ["Wedi i Rufain ymadael, ymladdodd y Brythoniaid dros eu hynys yn erbyn y Saeson. Mae Gildas, yn ysgrifennu yn y chweched ganrif, yn sôn am warchae Mynydd Baddon, y fuddugoliaeth fawr a ddaeth â heddwch a oedd heb ei dorri yn ei ddydd ei hun.",
         "Mae Historia Brittonum, a ysgrifennwyd yng Nghymru yn y nawfed ganrif, yn enwi’r arweinydd: Arthur, a gariodd groes Crist ar ei ysgwyddau ac a enillodd ddeuddeg brwydr, a’r olaf ym Maddon. Mae traddodiad Cymru yn dal mai Athrwys ap Meurig ydoedd, brenin Gwent a Glywysing. Mae haneswyr yn dal i drafod lle bu Baddon a pha bryd."]),
    "Camlann": (
        ["The Annals of Wales, kept at St Davids, record under the year 537: ‘The strife of Camlann, in which Arthur and Medrawd fell.’ It is one of the oldest written mentions of Arthur, a plain line in a list of years.",
         "Welsh tradition holds that Arthur did not die but was carried away to heal of his wounds, and that he sleeps in a cave with his warriors, waiting for the day Wales needs him. Caves at Craig y Dinas, Ogof Arthur in Ynys Môn and Snowdonia all claim him. Places called Camlan can still be found in Meirionnydd."],
        ["Mae Annales Cambriae, a gadwyd yn Nhyddewi, yn cofnodi dan y flwyddyn 537: ‘Gwaith Camlan, lle syrthiodd Arthur a Medrawd.’ Dyma un o’r cyfeiriadau ysgrifenedig hynaf at Arthur, llinell syml mewn rhestr o flynyddoedd.",
         "Mae traddodiad Cymru yn dal na fu farw Arthur ond iddo gael ei gludo ymaith i wella o’i glwyfau, a’i fod yn cysgu mewn ogof gyda’i filwyr, yn aros am y dydd y bydd Cymru ei angen. Mae Craig y Dinas, Ogof Arthur ym Môn ac Eryri i gyd yn ei hawlio. Gellir dod o hyd i leoedd o’r enw Camlan ym Meirionnydd o hyd."]),
    "Death of Saint David": (
        ["Dewi founded his monastery in Glyn Rhosyn, in the far west of Dyfed. His monks lived plainly: they ploughed by hand, drank water and kept silence. People came from across Wales and beyond to learn from him.",
         "His last sermon, as his life records it, is the most loved saying in Wales: ‘Be joyful, keep the faith, and do the little things that you have heard and seen me do.’ He died on 1 March, now St David’s Day, and his church grew into the cathedral of St Davids, a place of pilgrimage for fourteen centuries."],
        ["Sefydlodd Dewi ei fynachlog yng Nglyn Rhosyn, ym mhen pellaf Dyfed. Roedd ei fynachod yn byw yn syml: aredig â llaw, yfed dŵr a chadw distawrwydd. Daeth pobl o bob rhan o Gymru a thu hwnt i ddysgu ganddo.",
         "Ei bregeth olaf, fel y cofnodir hi yn ei fuchedd, yw’r dywediad anwylaf yng Nghymru: ‘Byddwch lawen a chedwch y ffydd, a gwnewch y pethau bychain a welsoch ac a glywsoch gennyf i.’ Bu farw ar 1 Mawrth, Dydd Gŵyl Dewi erbyn hyn, a thyfodd ei eglwys yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Tyddewi, cyrchfan pererinion ers pedair canrif ar ddeg."]),
    "Y Gododdin": (
        ["Y Gododdin was made by the poet Aneirin in the Old North, the Brittonic kingdoms of what is now southern Scotland and northern England. It mourns the warriors of Gododdin, around Din Eidyn (Edinburgh), who rode south to fight at Catraeth, probably Catterick, and fell almost to the last man.",
         "Each verse remembers a single hero: his courage, his generosity in the hall, the mead he earned. It survives in the Book of Aneirin, a manuscript of about 1265 now in Cardiff. Written in the language that became Welsh, it is among the oldest poetry of any language spoken in Britain today."],
        ["Canwyd Y Gododdin gan y bardd Aneirin yn yr Hen Ogledd, teyrnasoedd Brythonaidd yr hyn sydd heddiw’n dde’r Alban a gogledd Lloegr. Mae’n galaru am ryfelwyr Gododdin, o gwmpas Din Eidyn (Caeredin), a farchogodd i’r de i ymladd yng Nghatraeth, Catterick yn ôl pob tebyg, a syrthio bron i’r olaf.",
         "Mae pob pennill yn cofio un arwr: ei ddewrder, ei haelioni yn y neuadd, y medd a enillodd. Cedwir ef yn Llyfr Aneirin, llawysgrif o tua 1265 sydd bellach yng Nghaerdydd. Wedi’i ysgrifennu yn yr iaith a ddaeth yn Gymraeg, mae ymhlith y farddoniaeth hynaf yn unrhyw iaith a siaredir ym Mhrydain heddiw."]),
    "Offa’s Dyke": (
        ["Offa, King of Mercia, had a great bank and ditch dug along the edge of his lands facing Powys and Gwent. In places it still stands more than 20 metres wide from ditch bottom to bank top, and long stretches run between the Dee and the Severn.",
         "It was built to hold back the Welsh kingdoms, who had raided deep into Mercia. Twelve centuries later the border between Wales and England still follows it closely in places, and the Offa’s Dyke Path, opened in 1971, lets walkers follow the line from Prestatyn to Sedbury Cliffs."],
        ["Cododd Offa, Brenin Mersia, glawdd a ffos fawr ar hyd ymyl ei diroedd yn wynebu Powys a Gwent. Mewn mannau mae’n dal dros 20 metr o led o waelod y ffos i ben y clawdd, ac mae darnau hir yn rhedeg rhwng afon Dyfrdwy a’r Hafren.",
         "Fe’i codwyd i ddal teyrnasoedd Cymru yn ôl, a oedd wedi ymosod yn ddwfn i Fersia. Ddeuddeg canrif yn ddiweddarach mae’r ffin rhwng Cymru a Lloegr yn dal i’w ddilyn yn agos mewn mannau, ac mae Llwybr Clawdd Offa, a agorwyd yn 1971, yn gadael i gerddwyr ddilyn y llinell o Brestatyn i Glogwyni Sedbury."]),
    "Rhodri Mawr defeats the Vikings": (
        ["King Rhodri ap Merfyn ruled Gwynedd from 844, and by inheritance and marriage added Powys and Seisyllwg, so that he held most of Wales. When the Danes came to Môn in 856 he met them and killed their leader, Horm.",
         "The victory was celebrated far beyond Wales: the court of Charles the Bald in France heard of it, and an Irish scholar there wrote verses in Rhodri’s honour. No Viking kingdom took root in Wales. Rhodri is the first king remembered as Mawr, the Great, and his grandsons included Hywel Dda."],
        ["Teyrnasodd y Brenin Rhodri ap Merfyn ar Wynedd o 844, a thrwy etifeddiaeth a phriodas ychwanegodd Bowys a Seisyllwg, fel y daliai’r rhan fwyaf o Gymru. Pan ddaeth y Daniaid i Fôn yn 856 cyfarfu â hwy a lladd eu harweinydd, Horm.",
         "Dathlwyd y fuddugoliaeth ymhell y tu hwnt i Gymru: clywodd llys Siarl Foel yn Ffrainc amdani, ac ysgrifennodd ysgolhaig Gwyddelig yno gerddi er anrhydedd i Rhodri. Ni wreiddiodd unrhyw deyrnas Lychlynnaidd yng Nghymru. Rhodri yw’r brenin cyntaf a gofir fel Mawr, ac ymhlith ei wyrion yr oedd Hywel Dda."]),
    "The laws of Hywel Dda": (
        ["King Hywel ap Cadell, Hywel Dda, ruled almost all of Wales. Welsh tradition holds that he called six men from every cantref to his hunting lodge at Hendy-gwyn ar Daf, Whitland, in Lent, and that the wisest of them went through the old laws of Wales and set them in order.",
         "The laws that bear his name were kind for their age. They cared for the poor and the sick, gave women a share of property and the right to leave a marriage, and settled most wrongs with payment rather than punishment. They were copied in Welsh and Latin for centuries, and stayed in use until 1536."],
        ["Teyrnasodd y Brenin Hywel ap Cadell, Hywel Dda, ar bron y cyfan o Gymru. Mae traddodiad Cymru’n dal iddo alw chwe dyn o bob cantref i’w hendy hela yn Hendy-gwyn ar Daf yn y Grawys, ac i’r doethaf ohonynt fynd drwy hen gyfreithiau Cymru a’u rhoi mewn trefn.",
         "Roedd y cyfreithiau sy’n dwyn ei enw yn garedig ar gyfer eu hoes. Roedden nhw’n gofalu am y tlawd a’r claf, yn rhoi cyfran o eiddo i wragedd a’r hawl i adael priodas, ac yn setlo’r rhan fwyaf o gamweddau â thâl yn hytrach na chosb. Copïwyd hwy yn Gymraeg ac yn Lladin am ganrifoedd, a buont mewn grym hyd 1536."]),
    "One king for all Wales": (
        ["Gruffudd ap Llywelyn became King of Gwynedd and Powys in 1039, and in his first year he beat the English at Rhyd-y-groes on the Severn. By 1055 he had won Deheubarth and Morgannwg too, and for eight years he ruled all of Wales.",
         "He pushed the border east, took Hereford, and was recognised by the English crown as king of the Welsh. No other king before or after held all of Wales under one rule. After his death in 1063 the kingdoms parted again, but the idea of one Wales under one ruler lived on."],
        ["Daeth Gruffudd ap Llywelyn yn Frenin Gwynedd a Phowys yn 1039, ac yn ei flwyddyn gyntaf trechodd y Saeson yn Rhyd-y-groes ar Hafren. Erbyn 1055 roedd wedi ennill Deheubarth a Morgannwg hefyd, ac am wyth mlynedd teyrnasodd ar Gymru gyfan.",
         "Gwthiodd y ffin tua’r dwyrain, cipiodd Henffordd, ac fe’i cydnabuwyd gan goron Lloegr yn frenin y Cymry. Ni ddaliodd yr un brenin arall, cyn nac wedyn, Gymru gyfan dan un deyrnasiad. Wedi ei farw yn 1063 rhannodd y teyrnasoedd eto, ond parhaodd y syniad o un Gymru dan un rheolwr."]),
    "The Four Branches are written": (
        ["The Four Branches of the Mabinogi tell of Pwyll, Prince of Dyfed, and Rhiannon; of Branwen, daughter of Llŷr; of Manawydan; and of Math, Gwydion and Lleu. They were written down in Welsh around the end of the eleventh century by a storyteller whose name is not known.",
         "They survive in the White Book of Rhydderch and the Red Book of Hergest, and were brought to the world in English by Lady Charlotte Guest as the Mabinogion. With their shape-shifters, giants and wise women, set in real places from Harlech to Arberth, they are one of the great story cycles of medieval Europe."],
        ["Mae Pedair Cainc y Mabinogi yn adrodd am Bwyll, Pendefig Dyfed, a Rhiannon; am Franwen ferch Llŷr; am Fanawydan; ac am Fath, Gwydion a Lleu. Fe’u hysgrifennwyd yn Gymraeg tua diwedd yr unfed ganrif ar ddeg gan gyfarwydd nad yw ei enw’n hysbys.",
         "Fe’u cedwir yn Llyfr Gwyn Rhydderch a Llyfr Coch Hergest, a daethpwyd â hwy i’r byd yn Saesneg gan yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest fel y Mabinogion. Gyda’u newidwyr siâp, eu cewri a’u gwragedd doeth, wedi’u gosod mewn lleoedd go iawn o Harlech i Arberth, maent yn un o gylchoedd chwedlau mawr Ewrop yr Oesoedd Canol."]),
    "The king who became prince": (
        ["Owain Gwynedd was King of Gwynedd from 1137 and the strongest ruler in Wales. In 1157 and again in 1165 he faced the armies of Henry II, and in 1165 Henry’s great host was driven back from the Berwyn by rain, hunger and the Welsh.",
         "Owain then wrote to Louis VII of France as Waliarum princeps, Prince of Wales. Princeps was the title of Augustus, first among rulers, and it spoke for all the Welsh rather than one kingdom. It is the oldest surviving use of the title, and it was carried after him by the two Llywelyns and by Owain Glyndŵr."],
        ["Roedd Owain Gwynedd yn Frenin Gwynedd o 1137 a’r rheolwr cryfaf yng Nghymru. Yn 1157 ac eto yn 1165 wynebodd fyddinoedd Harri’r Ail, ac yn 1165 gyrrwyd llu mawr Harri yn ôl o’r Berwyn gan law, newyn a’r Cymry.",
         "Yna ysgrifennodd Owain at Louis VII o Ffrainc fel Waliarum princeps, Tywysog Cymru. Teitl Awgwstws oedd princeps, y cyntaf ymhlith rheolwyr, ac roedd yn siarad dros y Cymry i gyd yn hytrach nag un deyrnas. Dyma’r defnydd hynaf o’r teitl sydd wedi goroesi, a’i gario ar ei ôl gan y ddau Lywelyn a chan Owain Glyndŵr."]),
    "The first eisteddfod": (
        ["At Christmas 1176 the Lord Rhys, prince of Deheubarth, held a great gathering at his castle in Cardigan. It had been announced a year ahead throughout Wales and beyond. Two chairs were offered: one for poetry and one for music.",
         "The Chronicle of the Princes records that the chair for poetry went to the men of Gwynedd, and the chair for music to a young man of Rhys’s own court. It is the first eisteddfod on record, and the tradition of chairing the bard still crowns the National Eisteddfod every August."],
        ["Adeg y Nadolig 1176 cynhaliodd yr Arglwydd Rhys, tywysog Deheubarth, ŵyl fawr yn ei gastell yn Aberteifi. Fe’i cyhoeddwyd flwyddyn ymlaen llaw drwy Gymru a thu hwnt. Cynigiwyd dwy gadair: un am farddoniaeth ac un am gerddoriaeth.",
         "Mae Brut y Tywysogion yn cofnodi i gadair y beirdd fynd i wŷr Gwynedd, a chadair y gerddoriaeth i ŵr ifanc o lys Rhys ei hun. Dyma’r eisteddfod gyntaf ar gof a chadw, ac mae traddodiad cadeirio’r bardd yn dal i goroni’r Eisteddfod Genedlaethol bob mis Awst."]),
    "Death at Cilmeri": (
        ["Llywelyn ap Gruffudd was the only native Welsh ruler ever recognised by the English crown as Prince of Wales, in the Treaty of Montgomery of 1267. In 1282, when Wales rose again, he went south to rally support in the middle March.",
         "On 11 December, near Cilmeri by Builth, he was cut off from his army and killed. His head was sent to London. The poet Gruffudd ab yr Ynad Coch mourned him in one of the greatest elegies in Welsh: ‘Oh God, that the sea would cover the land!’ A stone at Cilmeri marks the place, and people gather there every December to remember Llywelyn Ein Llyw Olaf, Our Last Leader."],
        ["Llywelyn ap Gruffudd oedd yr unig reolwr brodorol a gydnabuwyd erioed gan goron Lloegr yn Dywysog Cymru, yng Nghytundeb Trefaldwyn 1267. Yn 1282, pan gododd Cymru eto, aeth i’r de i gasglu cefnogaeth yn y Mers canol.",
         "Ar 11 Rhagfyr, ger Cilmeri wrth Lanfair-ym-Muallt, fe’i torrwyd i ffwrdd o’i fyddin a’i ladd. Anfonwyd ei ben i Lundain. Galarodd y bardd Gruffudd ab yr Ynad Coch amdano yn un o’r marwnadau mwyaf yn Gymraeg: ‘Poni welwch-chwi’r môr yn merwino’r tir?’ Mae carreg yng Nghilmeri yn nodi’r fan, a bydd pobl yn ymgynnull yno bob mis Rhagfyr i gofio Llywelyn Ein Llyw Olaf."]),
    "Glyndŵr’s rising": (
        ["On 16 September 1400, at his home at Glyndyfrdwy, Owain Glyndŵr was proclaimed Prince of Wales by his family and friends. Within days the towns of the north east were in his hands, and the whole country rose with him.",
         "By 1404 he held most of Wales. He took Harlech and Aberystwyth castles, called parliaments at Machynlleth, made a treaty with France, and in the Pennal letter set out his plan for a Welsh church and two universities, one in the north and one in the south. For a few shining years Wales was free again."],
        ["Ar 16 Medi 1400, yn ei gartref yng Nglyndyfrdwy, cyhoeddwyd Owain Glyndŵr yn Dywysog Cymru gan ei deulu a’i gyfeillion. O fewn dyddiau roedd trefi’r gogledd-ddwyrain yn ei ddwylo, a chododd y wlad gyfan gydag ef.",
         "Erbyn 1404 daliai’r rhan fwyaf o Gymru. Cipiodd gestyll Harlech ac Aberystwyth, galwodd seneddau ym Machynlleth, gwnaeth gytundeb â Ffrainc, ac yn Llythyr Pennal gosododd ei gynllun ar gyfer eglwys Gymreig a dwy brifysgol, un yn y gogledd ac un yn y de. Am ychydig flynyddoedd disglair roedd Cymru’n rhydd eto."]),
    "Owain is never taken": (
        ["After Harlech fell in 1409, Owain Glyndŵr fought on from the hills. Twice he was offered a pardon by the king, and twice he did not take it. After 1415 nothing more is heard of him. He was never captured, and no one betrayed him.",
         "One chronicler wrote that very many said he died, but the seers said he did not. Welsh tradition holds that he sleeps with his men until Wales needs him again. His flag of four lions and the date of his rising, 16 September, Owain Glyndŵr Day, are still honoured across Wales."],
        ["Wedi i Harlech syrthio yn 1409, ymladdodd Owain Glyndŵr ymlaen o’r bryniau. Ddwywaith cynigiwyd pardwn iddo gan y brenin, a dwywaith ni chymerodd ef. Wedi 1415 ni chlywir dim mwy amdano. Ni chafodd ei ddal erioed, ac ni fradychodd neb ef.",
         "Ysgrifennodd un croniclydd fod llawer iawn yn dweud iddo farw, ond fod y daroganwyr yn dweud na wnaeth. Mae traddodiad Cymru’n dal ei fod yn cysgu gyda’i wŷr hyd nes y bydd Cymru ei angen eto. Mae ei faner o bedwar llew a dyddiad ei wrthryfel, 16 Medi, Diwrnod Owain Glyndŵr, yn dal i gael eu hanrhydeddu ar draws Cymru."]),
    "A Welsh king": (
        ["Harri Tudur was born in Pembroke Castle in 1457, grandson of Owain Tudur of Penmynydd in Môn. He spent fourteen years in exile in Brittany and France, and the poets of Wales sang of him as y mab darogan, the son of prophecy, who would restore the crown of Britain to the Britons.",
         "On 7 August 1485 he landed at Mill Bay near Dale with a small army. He marched north through Cardigan and Machynlleth and east to Shrewsbury, and the men of Wales came to him on the way. He carried the red dragon of Cadwaladr before him."],
        ["Ganwyd Harri Tudur yng Nghastell Penfro yn 1457, ŵyr Owain Tudur o Benmynydd ym Môn. Treuliodd bedair blynedd ar ddeg yn alltud yn Llydaw a Ffrainc, a chanai beirdd Cymru amdano fel y mab darogan, a fyddai’n adfer coron Prydain i’r Brythoniaid.",
         "Ar 7 Awst 1485 glaniodd ym Mae’r Felin ger Dale gyda byddin fechan. Gorymdeithiodd i’r gogledd drwy Aberteifi a Machynlleth ac i’r dwyrain i’r Amwythig, a daeth gwŷr Cymru ato ar y ffordd. Cariai ddraig goch Cadwaladr o’i flaen."]),
    "Victory at Bosworth": (
        ["On 22 August 1485 Harri Tudur met Richard III near Market Bosworth. Rhys ap Thomas of Dinefwr had brought the men of the south west, and the red dragon flew over the Welsh ranks. Richard was killed, and Harri was crowned Henry VII.",
         "To the Welsh poets the prophecy had come true: a king of the old British line sat on the throne of London. Henry named his first son Arthur, and the Tudor dynasty he founded, with its red dragon in the royal arms, ruled for more than a century."],
        ["Ar 22 Awst 1485 cyfarfu Harri Tudur â Rhisiart III ger Market Bosworth. Roedd Rhys ap Thomas o Ddinefwr wedi dod â gwŷr y de-orllewin, a chwifiai’r ddraig goch uwch rhengoedd y Cymry. Lladdwyd Rhisiart, a choronwyd Harri yn Harri VII.",
         "I feirdd Cymru roedd y broffwydoliaeth wedi’i chyflawni: roedd brenin o’r hen linach Frythonaidd ar orsedd Llundain. Enwodd Harri ei fab cyntaf yn Arthur, a theyrnasodd y llinach Duduraidd a sefydlodd, gyda’r ddraig goch yn arfau’r brenin, am dros ganrif."]),
    "The Laws in Wales Acts": (
        ["The Acts of 1536 and 1543 joined Wales to England in law. The Marcher lordships were swept away, Wales was divided into thirteen shires, and Welsh members were sent to Parliament for the first time.",
         "The same Acts ruled that no one who used the Welsh language could hold public office, and English became the language of the courts. Yet Welsh lived on in the homes, the chapels and the poetry of Wales, and within fifty years it had its own Bible. The language clause was not repealed until 1993."],
        ["Unodd Deddfau 1536 a 1543 Gymru â Lloegr yn y gyfraith. Diddymwyd arglwyddiaethau’r Mers, rhannwyd Cymru’n dair sir ar ddeg, ac anfonwyd aelodau o Gymru i’r Senedd am y tro cyntaf.",
         "Dyfarnodd yr un Deddfau na châi neb a ddefnyddiai’r Gymraeg ddal swydd gyhoeddus, a daeth Saesneg yn iaith y llysoedd. Eto i gyd parhaodd y Gymraeg yng nghartrefi, capeli a barddoniaeth Cymru, ac o fewn hanner can mlynedd roedd ganddi ei Beibl ei hun. Ni ddiddymwyd y cymal iaith tan 1993."]),
    "The equals sign": (
        ["Robert Recorde was born in Tenby in about 1512 and studied at Oxford and Cambridge. He wrote the first books of mathematics in English, so that ordinary people could learn arithmetic, geometry and astronomy without Latin.",
         "In The Whetstone of Witte of 1557 he grew tired of writing ‘is equal to’ again and again, and drew two parallel lines instead, ‘bicause noe 2 thynges can be moare equalle’. Every sum written anywhere in the world since uses the sign a man from Tenby invented."],
        ["Ganwyd Robert Recorde yn Ninbych-y-pysgod tua 1512 ac astudiodd yn Rhydychen a Chaergrawnt. Ysgrifennodd y llyfrau mathemateg cyntaf yn Saesneg, er mwyn i bobl gyffredin ddysgu rhifyddeg, geometreg a seryddiaeth heb Ladin.",
         "Yn The Whetstone of Witte yn 1557 blinodd ar ysgrifennu ‘is equal to’ dro ar ôl tro, a thynnodd ddwy linell gyfochrog yn lle hynny, ‘oherwydd na all dim dau beth fod yn fwy cyfartal’. Mae pob swm a ysgrifennwyd yn unman yn y byd ers hynny yn defnyddio’r arwydd a ddyfeisiodd gŵr o Ddinbych-y-pysgod."]),
    "The Welsh Bible": (
        ["William Morgan, born at Tŷ Mawr Wybrnant near Penmachno, was vicar of Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant when he translated the whole Bible into Welsh. Queen Elizabeth’s Parliament had ordered a Welsh Bible in 1563, and he finished the work in 1588.",
         "His Welsh was rich and dignified, drawn from the language of the poets, and it became the standard for written Welsh. With a Bible in every parish church, Welsh became a language of worship, learning and print, and it survived when the other Brittonic languages of the island did not."],
        ["Roedd William Morgan, a aned yn Nhŷ Mawr Wybrnant ger Penmachno, yn ficer Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant pan gyfieithodd y Beibl cyfan i’r Gymraeg. Roedd Senedd y Frenhines Elisabeth wedi gorchymyn Beibl Cymraeg yn 1563, a gorffennodd y gwaith yn 1588.",
         "Roedd ei Gymraeg yn gyfoethog ac urddasol, wedi’i thynnu o iaith y beirdd, a daeth yn safon i’r Gymraeg ysgrifenedig. Gyda Beibl ym mhob eglwys blwyf, daeth y Gymraeg yn iaith addoli, dysg a phrint, a goroesodd pan na wnaeth ieithoedd Brythonaidd eraill yr ynys."]),
    "The first railway locomotive": (
        ["On 21 February 1804 a steam engine built by the Cornish engineer Richard Trevithick hauled ten tons of iron, five wagons and some seventy men along the tramroad from Penydarren ironworks in Merthyr Tydfil to Abercynon, nearly ten miles.",
         "It was the first time a locomotive had run on rails anywhere in the world. Merthyr was then the iron capital of the world, and the railways that grew from that journey would carry Welsh coal, slate and iron to every continent."],
        ["Ar 21 Chwefror 1804 tynnodd peiriant stêm a adeiladwyd gan y peiriannydd o Gernyw Richard Trevithick ddeg tunnell o haearn, pum wagen a rhyw saith deg o ddynion ar hyd y dramffordd o waith haearn Penydarren ym Merthyr Tudful i Abercynon, bron i ddeng milltir.",
         "Dyma’r tro cyntaf i injan redeg ar gledrau yn unman yn y byd. Merthyr oedd prifddinas haearn y byd ar y pryd, a byddai’r rheilffyrdd a dyfodd o’r daith honno’n cludo glo, llechi a haearn Cymru i bob cyfandir."]),
    "The Merthyr Rising": (
        ["In June 1831 the iron workers of Merthyr Tydfil rose against cuts in their wages and the debtors’ court that took their goods. They held the town for several days, and a flag dipped in blood was raised as their banner: the first time the red flag was flown for working people in Britain.",
         "Soldiers were sent in and the rising was put down. Dic Penderyn, a young miner, was hanged in Cardiff for wounding a soldier, though many believed him innocent; his last words were ‘O Arglwydd, dyma gamwedd’, ‘O Lord, here is iniquity’. A plaque at Cardiff market marks the place, and he is remembered as a martyr of the working people of Wales."],
        ["Ym Mehefin 1831 cododd gweithwyr haearn Merthyr Tudful yn erbyn toriadau yn eu cyflogau a’r llys dyledion a gymerai eu heiddo. Daliasant y dref am sawl diwrnod, a chodwyd baner wedi’i throchi mewn gwaed fel eu lluman: y tro cyntaf i’r faner goch gael ei chwifio dros weithwyr ym Mhrydain.",
         "Anfonwyd milwyr i mewn a rhoddwyd diwedd ar y gwrthryfel. Crogwyd Dic Penderyn, glöwr ifanc, yng Nghaerdydd am glwyfo milwr, er i lawer gredu ei fod yn ddieuog; ei eiriau olaf oedd ‘O Arglwydd, dyma gamwedd’. Mae plac ym marchnad Caerdydd yn nodi’r fan, ac fe’i cofir fel merthyr gweithwyr Cymru."]),
    "The fuel cell": (
        ["William Robert Grove was born in Swansea in 1811. A judge by profession and a scientist by passion, he found that hydrogen and oxygen could be joined to make electricity and water, and in 1842 he built his ‘gas voltaic battery’ to show it.",
         "It was the first fuel cell. For a century it was a curiosity, until fuel cells powered the Apollo spacecraft that took people to the Moon. Today they drive buses, cars and power stations, and Grove of Swansea is honoured as the father of the fuel cell."],
        ["Ganwyd William Robert Grove yn Abertawe yn 1811. Barnwr wrth ei alwedigaeth a gwyddonydd o ran ei angerdd, darganfu y gellid uno hydrogen ac ocsigen i wneud trydan a dŵr, ac yn 1842 adeiladodd ei ‘fatri foltaidd nwy’ i ddangos hynny.",
         "Dyna’r gell danwydd gyntaf. Am ganrif bu’n chwilfrydedd, hyd nes i gelloedd tanwydd yrru’r llongau gofod Apollo a aeth â phobl i’r Lleuad. Heddiw maent yn gyrru bysiau, ceir a gorsafoedd pŵer, ac anrhydeddir Grove o Abertawe fel tad y gell danwydd."]),
    "Natural selection": (
        ["Alfred Russel Wallace was born in Usk in 1823 and learned his science as a young land surveyor around Neath, collecting beetles in the Vale of Neath. He went on to spend years collecting in the Amazon and the islands of South East Asia.",
         "In 1858, ill with fever on the island of Ternate, he worked out how species change through natural selection, and sent his paper to Charles Darwin. Their two papers were read together in London on 1 July 1858. Wallace is honoured as co-discoverer of evolution, and the line between the animals of Asia and Australia still bears his name."],
        ["Ganwyd Alfred Russel Wallace ym Mrynbuga yn 1823 a dysgodd ei wyddoniaeth fel tirfesurydd ifanc o gwmpas Castell-nedd, yn casglu chwilod yng Nghwm Nedd. Aeth ymlaen i dreulio blynyddoedd yn casglu yn yr Amazon ac ynysoedd De-ddwyrain Asia.",
         "Yn 1858, yn sâl gan dwymyn ar ynys Ternate, gweithiodd allan sut y mae rhywogaethau’n newid drwy ddethol naturiol, ac anfonodd ei bapur at Charles Darwin. Darllenwyd eu dau bapur gyda’i gilydd yn Llundain ar 1 Gorffennaf 1858. Anrhydeddir Wallace fel cyd-ddarganfyddwr esblygiad, ac mae’r llinell rhwng anifeiliaid Asia ac Awstralia yn dal i ddwyn ei enw."]),
    "The Welsh Not": (
        ["In the nineteenth century many schools in Wales taught only in English, and some used a piece of wood on a cord, marked ‘WN’, to stop children speaking Welsh. A child heard speaking Welsh was made to wear it and pass it on to the next, and whoever wore it at the end of the day was punished.",
         "The Blue Books of 1847 had blamed the Welsh language for the country’s troubles, and many parents came to believe that English was the way to get on. Yet the language was not beaten. It lived on in the chapels and Sunday schools, in the papers and the eisteddfodau, and the children who wore the Not grew up to speak Welsh to their own children."],
        ["Yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg dysgai llawer o ysgolion yng Nghymru drwy’r Saesneg yn unig, a defnyddiai rhai ddarn o bren ar linyn, wedi’i farcio ‘WN’, i atal plant rhag siarad Cymraeg. Gorfodid plentyn a glywyd yn siarad Cymraeg i’w wisgo a’i basio i’r nesaf, a chosbid pwy bynnag a’i gwisgai ar ddiwedd y dydd.",
         "Roedd Llyfrau Gleision 1847 wedi beio’r Gymraeg am drafferthion y wlad, a daeth llawer o rieni i gredu mai Saesneg oedd y ffordd i ddod ymlaen. Eto ni orchfygwyd yr iaith. Bu fyw yn y capeli a’r ysgolion Sul, yn y papurau a’r eisteddfodau, a thyfodd y plant a wisgodd y Not i siarad Cymraeg â’u plant eu hunain."]),
    "The first Grand Slam": (
        ["The years from 1900 to 1911 were the first golden age of Welsh rugby. Wales won the Triple Crown six times, and in 1905 beat the touring New Zealand All Blacks 3–0 at Cardiff Arms Park, the only side to beat them on that tour.",
         "In 1908 Wales beat England, Scotland, Ireland and France in a single season, the first Grand Slam, and did it again in 1909 and 1911. Rugby became the game of the valleys and the villages, and a source of pride that has never faded."],
        ["Y blynyddoedd o 1900 i 1911 oedd oes aur gyntaf rygbi Cymru. Enillodd Cymru’r Goron Driphlyg chwe gwaith, ac yn 1905 curodd Grysau Duon Seland Newydd 3–0 ar Barc yr Arfau, yr unig dîm i’w curo ar y daith honno.",
         "Yn 1908 curodd Cymru Loegr, yr Alban, Iwerddon a Ffrainc mewn un tymor, y Gamp Lawn gyntaf, a gwnaeth hynny eto yn 1909 ac 1911. Daeth rygbi’n gêm y cymoedd a’r pentrefi, ac yn ffynhonnell balchder nad yw erioed wedi pylu."]),
    "Senghenydd": (
        ["On the morning of 14 October 1913 an explosion tore through the Universal Colliery at Senghenydd, near Caerphilly. 439 men and boys were killed, the worst mining disaster in British history. Almost every family in the village lost someone.",
         "The same year the coalfield of south Wales reached its peak, with more than 230,000 miners. The Welsh National Mining Memorial at Senghenydd, opened on the hundredth anniversary, remembers the men of Senghenydd and of every Welsh pit disaster."],
        ["Fore 14 Hydref 1913 rhwygodd ffrwydrad drwy Lofa’r Universal yn Senghennydd, ger Caerffili. Lladdwyd 439 o ddynion a bechgyn, y trychineb glofaol gwaethaf yn hanes Prydain. Collodd bron pob teulu yn y pentref rywun.",
         "Yr un flwyddyn cyrhaeddodd maes glo’r de ei anterth, gyda dros 230,000 o lowyr. Mae Cofeb Genedlaethol y Glowyr yn Senghennydd, a agorwyd ar y canmlwyddiant, yn cofio gwŷr Senghennydd a phob trychineb mewn pwll yng Nghymru."]),
    "The black chair": (
        ["Ellis Humphrey Evans, known by his bardic name Hedd Wyn, was a shepherd and poet from Yr Ysgwrn, a farm near Trawsfynydd. He sent his awdl ‘Yr Arwr’, The Hero, to the National Eisteddfod in the summer of 1917, and was killed at Pilckem Ridge in Flanders on 31 July.",
         "On 6 September, at Birkenhead, the Archdruid called three times for the winning poet to stand. No one rose, and the chair was draped in a black sheet. The Black Chair stands today at Yr Ysgwrn, and Hedd Wyn speaks for a whole generation of young Welshmen lost in the war."],
        ["Bugail a bardd o’r Ysgwrn, fferm ger Trawsfynydd, oedd Ellis Humphrey Evans, a adwaenid wrth ei enw barddol Hedd Wyn. Anfonodd ei awdl ‘Yr Arwr’ i’r Eisteddfod Genedlaethol yn haf 1917, a lladdwyd ef yng Nghefn Pilckem yn Fflandrys ar 31 Gorffennaf.",
         "Ar 6 Medi, ym Mhenbedw, galwodd yr Archdderwydd deirgwaith ar y bardd buddugol i sefyll. Ni chododd neb, a thaenwyd llen ddu dros y gadair. Mae’r Gadair Ddu i’w gweld heddiw yn Yr Ysgwrn, ac mae Hedd Wyn yn llefaru dros genhedlaeth gyfan o fechgyn ifanc Cymru a gollwyd yn y rhyfel."]),
    "The NHS": (
        ["In Tredegar, where Aneurin Bevan grew up, the miners and steelworkers paid a few pence a week into the Tredegar Medical Aid Society, and in return the whole family had doctors, nurses and medicine free when they needed them.",
         "As Minister of Health, Bevan took that idea to the whole of Britain. On 5 July 1948 the National Health Service opened, free to all at the point of use. ‘We are going to Tredegarise you,’ he is remembered as saying. It is one of the greatest gifts Wales has given the world."],
        ["Yn Nhredegar, lle magwyd Aneurin Bevan, talai’r glowyr a’r gweithwyr dur ychydig geiniogau’r wythnos i Gymdeithas Cymorth Meddygol Tredegar, ac yn gyfnewid câi’r teulu cyfan feddygon, nyrsys a moddion am ddim pan fyddai eu hangen.",
         "Fel Gweinidog Iechyd, aeth Bevan â’r syniad hwnnw i Brydain gyfan. Ar 5 Gorffennaf 1948 agorodd y Gwasanaeth Iechyd Gwladol, am ddim i bawb pan fo’i angen. ‘Rydyn ni’n mynd i’ch Tredegareiddio chi,’ yw’r dywediad a gofir amdano. Dyma un o’r rhoddion mwyaf a roddodd Cymru i’r byd."]),
    "The red dragon made official": (
        ["The red dragon is one of the oldest emblems of the Britons. The History of the Britons tells of the red dragon of the Britons and the white dragon of the Saxons fighting beneath Dinas Emrys, and Welsh tradition gives the red dragon to Cadwaladr, last king of the Britons. Harri Tudur flew it at Bosworth, and the Tudors set it in the royal arms.",
         "The green and white behind it are the Tudor colours. In 1959 the Queen approved the red dragon on green and white as the official flag of Wales. It is one of the very few national flags with a dragon on it, and one of the best loved."],
        ["Mae’r ddraig goch yn un o arwyddluniau hynaf y Brythoniaid. Mae Historia Brittonum yn adrodd am ddraig goch y Brythoniaid a draig wen y Saeson yn ymladd o dan Ddinas Emrys, ac mae traddodiad Cymru’n rhoi’r ddraig goch i Gadwaladr, brenin olaf y Brythoniaid. Chwifiodd Harri Tudur hi yn Bosworth, a gosododd y Tuduriaid hi yn arfau’r brenin.",
         "Lliwiau’r Tuduriaid yw’r gwyrdd a’r gwyn y tu ôl iddi. Yn 1959 cymeradwyodd y Frenhines y ddraig goch ar wyrdd a gwyn fel baner swyddogol Cymru. Mae’n un o’r ychydig iawn o faneri cenedlaethol â draig arni, ac yn un o’r anwylaf."]),
    "Tryweryn": (
        ["Capel Celyn was a Welsh-speaking village in the Tryweryn valley near Bala, with a chapel, a school, a post office and a dozen farms. In 1957 Liverpool Corporation won an Act of Parliament to drown the valley for a reservoir. Thirty-five of the thirty-six Welsh MPs voted against it, and the villagers marched through Liverpool to plead for their homes.",
         "The valley was flooded and Llyn Celyn opened in 1965. The fight for Tryweryn woke a nation: it fed the growth of Plaid Cymru and the campaign for a Welsh parliament. The words ‘Cofiwch Dryweryn’, Remember Tryweryn, painted on a wall near Llanrhystud, are copied on walls across Wales and the world."],
        ["Pentref Cymraeg yng Nghwm Tryweryn ger y Bala oedd Capel Celyn, gyda chapel, ysgol, swyddfa bost a dwsin o ffermydd. Yn 1957 enillodd Corfforaeth Lerpwl Ddeddf Seneddol i foddi’r cwm ar gyfer cronfa ddŵr. Pleidleisiodd tri deg pump o’r tri deg chwech o Aelodau Seneddol Cymru yn ei herbyn, a gorymdeithiodd y pentrefwyr drwy Lerpwl i ymbil dros eu cartrefi.",
         "Boddwyd y cwm ac agorwyd Llyn Celyn yn 1965. Deffrodd y frwydr dros Dryweryn genedl: bwydodd dwf Plaid Cymru a’r ymgyrch dros senedd i Gymru. Mae’r geiriau ‘Cofiwch Dryweryn’, a baentiwyd ar wal ger Llanrhystud, wedi’u copïo ar waliau ar draws Cymru a’r byd."]),
    "Aberfan": (
        ["At a quarter past nine on the morning of Friday 21 October 1966, the last day before the half-term holiday, a coal waste tip on the mountain above Aberfan slid down onto Pantglas Junior School and the houses beside it. 144 people died, 116 of them children.",
         "Miners, neighbours and volunteers came from across Wales to dig through the day and the night. The village has never forgotten. The children are remembered in the garden of remembrance where the school stood and in the cemetery on the hillside, and Aberfan’s dignity in its grief is honoured throughout the world."],
        ["Am chwarter wedi naw fore Gwener 21 Hydref 1966, y diwrnod olaf cyn gwyliau hanner tymor, llithrodd tomen wastraff glo ar y mynydd uwchben Aberfan i lawr ar Ysgol Gynradd Pantglas a’r tai wrth ei hymyl. Bu farw 144 o bobl, 116 ohonynt yn blant.",
         "Daeth glowyr, cymdogion a gwirfoddolwyr o bob rhan o Gymru i gloddio drwy’r dydd a’r nos. Nid yw’r pentref erioed wedi anghofio. Cofir y plant yn yr ardd goffa lle safai’r ysgol ac yn y fynwent ar lethr y bryn, ac anrhydeddir urddas Aberfan yn ei galar drwy’r byd."]),
    "Gwynfor and S4C": (
        ["In 1979 the government went back on its promise of a Welsh-language television channel. Gwynfor Evans, Plaid Cymru’s first MP, announced that from 6 October 1980 he would fast until the promise was kept, or until he died.",
         "Wales stood behind him, and in September 1980 the government gave way. S4C, Sianel Pedwar Cymru, began broadcasting on 1 November 1982, and its programmes, from Superted to Pobol y Cwm, carried Welsh into homes across the country."],
        ["Yn 1979 aeth y llywodraeth yn ôl ar ei haddewid o sianel deledu Gymraeg. Cyhoeddodd Gwynfor Evans, Aelod Seneddol cyntaf Plaid Cymru, y byddai’n ymprydio o 6 Hydref 1980 hyd nes y cedwid yr addewid, neu hyd nes y byddai farw.",
         "Safodd Cymru y tu ôl iddo, ac ym Medi 1980 ildiodd y llywodraeth. Dechreuodd S4C, Sianel Pedwar Cymru, ddarlledu ar 1 Tachwedd 1982, a chariodd ei rhaglenni, o Superted i Pobol y Cwm, y Gymraeg i gartrefi ar draws y wlad."]),
    "Yes to devolution": (
        ["Eighteen years after Wales had turned down an Assembly in 1979, it was asked again on 18 September 1997. The count went on through the night, and the last result, from Carmarthenshire, turned the vote: Yes won by 50.3 per cent, a majority of 6,721.",
         "The National Assembly for Wales first met on 12 May 1999, the first elected all-Wales government since the days of Owain Glyndŵr’s parliaments. In 2011 Wales voted by almost two to one to give it full law-making powers, and in 2006 it moved into the Senedd building on Cardiff Bay."],
        ["Ddeunaw mlynedd wedi i Gymru wrthod Cynulliad yn 1979, gofynnwyd iddi eto ar 18 Medi 1997. Aeth y cyfrif ymlaen drwy’r nos, a’r canlyniad olaf, o Sir Gaerfyrddin, a drodd y bleidlais: enillodd Ie o 50.3 y cant, mwyafrif o 6,721.",
         "Cyfarfu Cynulliad Cenedlaethol Cymru gyntaf ar 12 Mai 1999, y llywodraeth etholedig gyntaf dros Gymru gyfan ers dyddiau seneddau Owain Glyndŵr. Yn 2011 pleidleisiodd Cymru o bron i ddau i un i roi pwerau deddfu llawn iddo, ac yn 2006 symudodd i adeilad y Senedd ym Mae Caerdydd."]),
    "Half and half": (
        ["At the Assembly election of May 2003, thirty women and thirty men were elected to the sixty seats. No national parliament anywhere in the world had ever had as many women as men before.",
         "It came from the parties’ own choices, such as twinning seats so that a man and a woman were chosen side by side, and it made Wales a model studied around the world."],
        ["Yn etholiad y Cynulliad ym Mai 2003, etholwyd deg ar hugain o fenywod a deg ar hugain o ddynion i’r chwe deg sedd. Nid oedd yr un senedd genedlaethol yn unman yn y byd erioed wedi cael cymaint o fenywod â dynion o’r blaen.",
         "Daeth o ddewisiadau’r pleidiau eu hunain, megis gefeillio seddau fel y dewisid dyn a menyw ochr yn ochr, a gwnaeth Gymru’n fodel a astudiwyd ar draws y byd."]),
    "The Wales Coast Path": (
        ["The Wales Coast Path opened on 5 May 2012. It runs 870 miles from Queensferry on the Dee to Chepstow on the Severn, past cliffs, castles, harbours and beaches, through two National Parks and around Ynys Môn and Llŷn.",
         "Wales became the first country in the world with a footpath along its whole coastline. Joined to the Offa’s Dyke Path along the border, it makes a walk of about 1,030 miles right around Wales."],
        ["Agorwyd Llwybr Arfordir Cymru ar 5 Mai 2012. Mae’n rhedeg 870 milltir o Queensferry ar afon Dyfrdwy i Gas-gwent ar yr Hafren, heibio i glogwyni, cestyll, harbwrs a thraethau, drwy ddau Barc Cenedlaethol ac o gwmpas Ynys Môn a Llŷn.",
         "Daeth Cymru’r wlad gyntaf yn y byd â llwybr troed ar hyd ei harfordir cyfan. O’i uno â Llwybr Clawdd Offa ar hyd y ffin, mae’n gwneud taith o tua 1,030 o filltiroedd o amgylch Cymru gyfan."]),
    "Senedd Cymru": (
        ["On 6 May 2020 the National Assembly for Wales took a new name: Senedd Cymru, the Welsh Parliament, known in both languages simply as the Senedd. The same law gave the vote in Senedd elections to sixteen and seventeen year olds.",
         "The name says what it had become: a parliament that makes the laws of Wales, from health and schools to the Welsh language. The word senedd has been used in Welsh for the councils of the land since the Middle Ages, and Owain Glyndŵr called his own at Machynlleth."],
        ["Ar 6 Mai 2020 cymerodd Cynulliad Cenedlaethol Cymru enw newydd: Senedd Cymru, Welsh Parliament, a adwaenir yn y ddwy iaith yn syml fel y Senedd. Rhoddodd yr un gyfraith y bleidlais yn etholiadau’r Senedd i bobl un ar bymtheg a dwy ar bymtheg oed.",
         "Mae’r enw’n dweud beth yr oedd wedi dod: senedd sy’n gwneud cyfreithiau Cymru, o iechyd ac ysgolion i’r Gymraeg. Defnyddiwyd y gair senedd yn Gymraeg am gynghorau’r wlad ers yr Oesoedd Canol, a galwodd Owain Glyndŵr ei senedd ei hun ym Machynlleth."]),
    "The slate landscape, World Heritage": (
        ["In the nineteenth century the quarries and mines of Gwynedd, at Penrhyn, Dinorwig, Blaenau Ffestiniog, Nantlle and the Ogwen valley, were the greatest slate industry in the world. Welsh slate roofed terraces, palaces and cathedrals from Melbourne to New York.",
         "On 28 July 2021 UNESCO named the Slate Landscape of Northwest Wales a World Heritage Site, the fourth in Wales. It honours the skill of the quarrymen, the Welsh-speaking villages and chapels they built, and the railways and harbours that carried their slate to the world."],
        ["Yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg chwareli a mwyngloddiau Gwynedd, yn y Penrhyn, Dinorwig, Blaenau Ffestiniog, Nantlle a Dyffryn Ogwen, oedd y diwydiant llechi mwyaf yn y byd. Toeodd llechi Cymru derasau, palasau ac eglwysi cadeiriol o Melbourne i Efrog Newydd.",
         "Ar 28 Gorffennaf 2021 enwodd UNESCO Dirwedd Llechi Gogledd-orllewin Cymru yn Safle Treftadaeth y Byd, y pedwerydd yng Nghymru. Mae’n anrhydeddu medr y chwarelwyr, y pentrefi a’r capeli Cymraeg a godasant, a’r rheilffyrdd a’r harbwrs a gludodd eu llechi i’r byd."]),
}
